+#### Efficient Signed Distance to Triangle Mesh
+A method to compute the signed distance from a 3D point to a triangle mesh efficiently using a BVH for unsigned distance and ray casting for sign determination [S4].
+1.  Construct a triangle BVH for efficient unsigned distance queries, achieving $O(\log N_{triangles})$ average complexity [S4].
+2.  Trace 32 'stab rays' distributed uniformly over the sphere using a Fibonacci lattice, pseudorandomly and independently offset for every training position [S4].
+3.  If any ray reaches infinity, mark the position as 'outside' and the distance as positive. Otherwise, mark it as negative [S4].
+4.  Use NVIDIA ray tracing hardware via the OptiX 7 framework for ray-triangle intersections to maximize efficiency [S4].
+*   Parameter: Number of stab rays: 32 [S4].
+*   Pitfall: For watertight meshes, signing distance based on the normal of the closest triangle is cheaper, but this is disabled by default due to incompatibility with typical non-watertight meshes found in the wild [S4].
+*   Comparison: Using OptiX 7 is over an order of magnitude faster than using a software triangle BVH for ray-shape intersections on an RTX 3090 GPU [S4].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Unsigned distance queries achieve $O(\log N_{triangles})$ average complexity [S4]. Ray tracing using OptiX 7 is over an order of magnitude faster than software triangle BVH for ray-shape intersections on an RTX 3090 GPU [S4].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the BVH construction; typically $O(N_{triangles})$ for storing nodes and leaves [S1, unofficial][S4].
-*   **Strengths:** High performance on GPU hardware, especially with dedicated ray tracing cores (OptiX) [S4]. Supports efficient distance queries and signing for neural rendering [S4].
-*   **Limitations:** The default signing method (stab rays) requires multiple ray casts per point, which can be costly if not optimized with hardware acceleration [S4]. The normal-based signing is incompatible with non-watertight meshes [S4].
-*   **When to Use:** When computing signed distances or performing ray intersections on large triangle meshes in a GPU-accelerated environment, particularly for neural rendering or SDF-based applications [S1, unofficial][S4].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For small meshes where the overhead of BVH construction outweighs the benefits, or when working with non-watertight meshes and requiring fast normal-based signing without the stab ray overhead (though the method disables this by default) [S4].
